f swiss that i  charge should go up so when the price of  cheddar swings like it did in 2020  so does the wholesale price of other  cheeses  again here's where bulk orders dried up  early last year and around here the  government started the farmers to family  food box program to help farmers weather  the storm it included a promise to buy a  hundred million dollars a month in dairy  products including cheese  all right thank you have a good day  there were several different types of  baskets that they authorized there was a  dairy basket and a milk basket and they  paid to acquire those products  put them in a paper bag or a cardboard  box  give them to local food distribution  centers we saw the price of cheese  absolutely explode  for a short period of time because there  was this requirement to get this huge  volume of cheese in a very narrow window  after a few months of shocks prices  returned to a bit lower than what they  were right before the pandemic as these  programs ran their course influencing  more sharp swings customers were picking  up vast quantities of cheese at stores  which brings us to the question at hand  why on average are these swings hard to  notice in the dairy aisle novakovich  attributes it in part to something  called price smoothing when prices are  increasing rapidly for some reason  grocery stores tend to say we don't want  to reflect all of that they anticipate  being able to recoup  that margin at some point when prices  recede so instead of  seeing quite the same roller coaster at  the retail level  generally consumers  see a much smoother  effect novakovich says customer  expectations can also influence pricing  strategies  take apples  because they're grown seasonally  customers may expect them to go up and  down throughout the year more than  something like cheese also cheese is  perishable customers may buy it more  frequently than other foods and would be  more likely to notice price changes  another incentive for grocers to keep  prices stable  while some in the cheese business have  had to weather big market swings  a smaller section of the industry has  managed to bypass much of the storm  aaron weiswall who owns an artisanal  creamery told us that while the cme was  swinging in 2020 his business was  thriving  2020 was one of our best years because  of farm markets and direct sales the  public did not want to go to retail  stores producing their own milk and  selling direct to consumers allows some  artisans to skip the wild wholesale  markets we are basically in farmstead  creamery which means we make most of our  own milk on site we age our cheeses in a  cave on site  and we original wisewell said that while  he's protected from some market  conditions 2021 has still presented  challenges 21 is rough because of labor  we're producing and selling everything  we make  but a few of us are working longer hours  to make the same stuff because we can't  find enough people to make it wisewell  said that while he's managed to hold  steady on prices so far  he's feeling this year's supply chain  pressures like much of the rest of the  economy the way we are doing it is  sometimes increasing the minimum  that the customer has to buy from us  changing delivery minimum increasing  delivery prices trying to hold that i  don't know how long we can hold them  if it continues the way it's continuing  it's gonna have  to get a boost  but uh we don't like to increase prices  uh and and uh we haven't probably  in cheese for almost five years  as the economy continues to see supply  and production challenges  the price of cheese on commodity markets  and in the store remains uncertain  you
 

